			float hairRotationZ = 0.0f;
			float hairOffsetX = -0.2f;
			float hairOffsetY = 0.0f;
			float hairOffsetZ = -2.2f;
			// Simplified hair-vs-head "collision": any hair node closer than
			// this to the hair-root centroid gets pushed straight out to
			// this distance. Not real collision (no per-frame head
			// tracking, no shape other than a sphere) — just enough to stop
			// the most visible clipping into the scalp. Tune via ini; the
			// right value depends on this game's coordinate scale, which we
			// don't know precisely, so this default is a starting guess.
			float hairHeadCollisionRadius = 15.0f;
			// When false (the stock AliceCoop behaviour), the proxy's cloth
			// is deliberately frozen: its simulator is deleted and physics
			// disabled, because the game's cloth simulator resolves "the"
			// playable Alice globally and would otherwise skin the proxy's
			// dress onto the LOCAL Alice's bones. That trade buys a stable
			// dress on the right body at the cost of looking rigid.
			// Setting this true keeps the cloth simulating — experimental,
			// and expected to reintroduce the original problem, but worth
			// being able to see for ourselves without a rebuild.
			bool proxyClothPhysics = false;
			// Diagnostic logs proved the proxy's HairComponent points at the
			// LOCAL Alice's Hair template object (e.g. both at
			// AlicePawn_3.Hair_15) while each has its own live simulator —
			// two independent simulations writing into one shared geometry,
			// which matches the reported corruption affecting both Alices at
			// once. When true, skip that Template assignment entirely and
			// let the proxy keep whatever Hair object the engine created for
			// it at spawn time.
			bool proxyOwnHairTemplate = false;
			// londonhairdup1: in the real world the proxy gets its own copy
